<section class="chapter" id="os-introduction">
<h1>Operating System Fundamentals</h1>
<p>
        An Operating System (OS) is foundational system software
        that manages computer hardware resources, provides services
        to application programs, and controls execution privileges
        in multi-user and multitasking environments.
    </p>
<p>
        The operating system acts as an intermediary between
        application software and computer hardware. It manages
        processes, memory, file systems, storage devices,
        input/output operations, and security.
    </p>
<h3>Operating System Structure</h3>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>+------------------------------------+
|          USER APPLICATIONS         |
|   Browsers, Games, Editors, IDEs   |
+------------------------------------+
|       OPERATING SYSTEM API         |
|     System Calls, Shell, GUI       |
+------------------------------------+
|          OPERATING SYSTEM          |
| Process, Memory, Files, I/O, etc.  |
+------------------------------------+
|          COMPUTER HARDWARE         |
|    CPU, RAM, Storage, Devices      |
+------------------------------------+</code></pre>
</div>
<h3>Core Objectives</h3>
<ul>
<li>Manage hardware resources efficiently.</li>
<li>Provide a convenient environment for applications.</li>
<li>Support multiple programs and users.</li>
<li>Enforce system protection and security.</li>
<li>Coordinate hardware and software operations.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-architecture">
<h2>1. Operating System Architecture and Kernel Structures</h2>
<p>
        Operating systems use different kernel architectures
        to organize essential services such as process
        management, memory allocation, device drivers,
        and file systems.
    </p>
<h3>Monolithic Kernel</h3>
<p>
        A monolithic kernel places core operating system
        services inside one privileged kernel address space.
        This design provides fast communication between
        services but a faulty kernel component can affect
        the entire system.
    </p>
<h3>Microkernel</h3>
<p>
        A microkernel keeps only essential functions,
        including low-level memory management,
        interprocess communication, and scheduling,
        inside the kernel.
    </p>
<p>
        Other services such as device drivers and file
        systems can run in user space, improving modularity
        and fault isolation.
    </p>
<h3>Hybrid Kernel</h3>
<p>
        A hybrid kernel combines elements of monolithic
        and microkernel architectures to balance
        performance and modularity.
    </p>
<h3>Exokernel</h3>
<p>
        An exokernel exposes hardware resources more directly
        to application-level library operating systems,
        enabling specialized resource management.
    </p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Kernel Type</th>
<th>Advantage</th>
<th>Limitation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Monolithic</td>
<td>Fast internal service communication</td>
<td>Limited fault isolation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Microkernel</td>
<td>Modularity and isolation</td>
<td>IPC overhead</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Hybrid</td>
<td>Performance and flexibility</td>
<td>Architectural complexity</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Exokernel</td>
<td>Application-specific optimization</td>
<td>Complex application development</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-execution-modes">
<h2>2. Hardware Abstraction and Execution Modes</h2>
<p>
        Computer hardware uses privilege levels to prevent
        applications from directly accessing protected
        system resources.
    </p>
<h3>User Mode</h3>
<p>
        User mode is the restricted execution environment
        used by ordinary applications. Programs cannot
        directly execute privileged instructions or
        access protected kernel memory.
    </p>
<h3>Kernel Mode</h3>
<p>
        Kernel mode allows the operating system to perform
        privileged operations, configure hardware controllers,
        and manage protected memory.
    </p>
<h3>Protection Rings</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ring 0:</strong> Kernel-level privilege.</li>
<li><strong>Ring 3:</strong> User-level privilege.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Traps and Privilege Transitions</h3>
<p>
        A system call can trigger a controlled transition
        from user mode to kernel mode through a CPU trap
        or dedicated system-call instruction.
    </p>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>USER APPLICATION
       |
       | System Call / Trap
       v
KERNEL MODE
       |
       | Execute Protected Service
       v
RETURN TO USER MODE</code></pre>
</div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-system-calls">
<h2>3. System Calls and Operating System APIs</h2>
<p>
        A system call is the interface through which
        applications request operating system services.
    </p>
<p>
        Applications commonly use API libraries such as
        POSIX and Win32 to access system functionality.
    </p>
<h3>System Call Execution</h3>
<ol>
<li>An application requests an operating system service.</li>
<li>A library wrapper prepares the request.</li>
<li>A system-call instruction enters kernel mode.</li>
<li>The kernel identifies and executes the service.</li>
<li>The result is returned to the application.</li>
</ol>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>Application
    |
    v
read(fd, buffer, size)
    |
    v
System Call Interface
    |
    v
Kernel Trap Handler
    |
    v
File System / Device Driver
    |
    v
Return Result</code></pre>
</div>
<h3>Examples of System Services</h3>
<ul>
<li>Creating and terminating processes.</li>
<li>Reading and writing files.</li>
<li>Allocating memory.</li>
<li>Communicating with devices.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-process-management">
<h2>4. Process Management: Concepts and Abstraction</h2>
<h3>What Is a Process?</h3>
<p>
        A process is a program currently in execution.
        Unlike a program stored on disk, a process
        has an active execution context, memory,
        and CPU state.
    </p>
<h3>Process Memory Layout</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Memory Region</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Text</td>
<td>Executable instructions</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Data</td>
<td>Initialized global and static variables</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>BSS</td>
<td>Uninitialized global and static variables</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Heap</td>
<td>Dynamic memory allocation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Stack</td>
<td>Function calls and local variables</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Process Control Block (PCB)</h3>
<p>
        The PCB stores information needed by the
        operating system to manage a process.
    </p>
<ul>
<li>Process ID (PID)</li>
<li>Current process state</li>
<li>Program counter</li>
<li>CPU registers</li>
<li>Memory management information</li>
<li>Open files and I/O information</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-process-scheduling">
<h2>5. Process Scheduling and Lifecycle</h2>
<p>
        Process scheduling determines when processes
        receive CPU time and how they move through
        their execution states.
    </p>
<h3>Process States</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>New:</strong> Process is being created.</li>
<li><strong>Ready:</strong> Waiting for CPU allocation.</li>
<li><strong>Running:</strong> Currently executing.</li>
<li><strong>Waiting:</strong> Waiting for an event or I/O operation.</li>
<li><strong>Terminated:</strong> Execution has finished.</li>
</ul>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>NEW --&gt; READY --&gt; RUNNING --&gt; TERMINATED
          ^          |
          |          v
          +------- WAITING
             I/O Complete</code></pre>
</div>
<h3>Context Switching</h3>
<p>
        A context switch occurs when the operating system
        saves the execution state of one process and
        restores the state of another.
    </p>
<h3>Types of Schedulers</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Long-Term:</strong> Controls admission of jobs.</li>
<li><strong>Medium-Term:</strong> Manages suspension and swapping.</li>
<li><strong>Short-Term:</strong> Selects the next ready process.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-cpu-scheduling">
<h2>6. CPU Scheduling Algorithms</h2>
<p>
        CPU scheduling algorithms determine which
        ready process should execute next.
    </p>
<h3>Scheduling Criteria</h3>
<ul>
<li>CPU Utilization</li>
<li>Throughput</li>
<li>Turnaround Time</li>
<li>Waiting Time</li>
<li>Response Time</li>
</ul>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Algorithm</th>
<th>Mechanism</th>
<th>Characteristic</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>FCFS</td>
<td>Arrival order</td>
<td>Simple; convoy effect possible</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SJF</td>
<td>Shortest CPU burst first</td>
<td>Low average waiting time</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SRTF</td>
<td>Shortest remaining time</td>
<td>Preemptive scheduling</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Round Robin</td>
<td>Fixed time quantum</td>
<td>Suitable for time-sharing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>MLFQ</td>
<td>Multiple priority queues</td>
<td>Adapts to process behavior</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Round-Robin Example</h3>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>Time Quantum: 2 ms

P1: 5 ms
P2: 3 ms
P3: 2 ms

Execution:
P1(2) -&gt; P2(2) -&gt; P3(2)
      -&gt; P1(2) -&gt; P2(1)
      -&gt; P1(1)</code></pre>
</div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-threads">
<h2>7. Threads and Concurrency Foundations</h2>
<p>
        A thread is a basic unit of CPU execution
        within a process. Multiple threads in the
        same process share memory and resources
        while maintaining separate execution contexts.
    </p>
<h3>Thread Components</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thread identifier</li>
<li>Program counter</li>
<li>Register set</li>
<li>Execution stack</li>
</ul>
<h3>Multithreading Models</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Model</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Many-to-One</td>
<td>Many user threads map to one kernel thread.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>One-to-One</td>
<td>Each user thread maps to a kernel thread.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Many-to-Many</td>
<td>User threads are multiplexed over kernel threads.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-race-conditions">
<h2>8. Process Synchronization and Race Conditions</h2>
<p>
        When multiple threads access shared data
        concurrently, the final result may depend
        on the order of execution.
    </p>
<h3>Race Condition</h3>
<p>
        A race condition occurs when concurrent
        operations access shared resources without
        adequate synchronization.
    </p>
<h3>Critical Section</h3>
<p>
        A critical section is the portion of code
        that accesses shared resources and must
        be protected from conflicting execution.
    </p>
<h3>Requirements for Correct Synchronization</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Mutual Exclusion:</strong> Only one process enters the protected section at a time.</li>
<li><strong>Progress:</strong> Eligible waiting processes can make progress.</li>
<li><strong>Bounded Waiting:</strong> A waiting process cannot be postponed indefinitely.</li>
</ol>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-synchronization">
<h2>9. Synchronization Primitives</h2>
<h3>Mutex Locks</h3>
<p>
        A mutex provides exclusive access to
        a shared resource using lock and unlock
        operations.
    </p>
<h3>Semaphores</h3>
<p>
        A semaphore is a synchronization primitive
        controlled by atomic wait and signal operations.
    </p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Binary Semaphore:</strong> Represents two logical states.</li>
<li><strong>Counting Semaphore:</strong> Manages multiple resource instances.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Semaphore Example</h3>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>wait(S):
    S.value = S.value - 1

    if S.value &lt; 0:
        block current process


signal(S):
    S.value = S.value + 1

    if S.value &lt;= 0:
        wake a waiting process</code></pre>
</div>
<p>
        The wait and signal operations must execute
        atomically to preserve synchronization.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-deadlocks">
<h2>10. Deadlocks: Characterization and Handling</h2>
<p>
        A deadlock occurs when two or more processes
        remain blocked indefinitely because each
        is waiting for a resource held by another.
    </p>
<h3>The Four Coffman Conditions</h3>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Mutual Exclusion:</strong>
            A resource cannot be shared simultaneously.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>Hold and Wait:</strong>
            A process holds resources while requesting others.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>No Preemption:</strong>
            Resources cannot be forcibly taken away.
        </li>
<li>
<strong>Circular Wait:</strong>
            Processes form a circular chain of resource dependencies.
        </li>
</ol>
<h3>Deadlock Example</h3>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>Process P1:
Holds Resource A
Waiting for Resource B

Process P2:
Holds Resource B
Waiting for Resource A

P1 --&gt; B --&gt; P2 --&gt; A --&gt; P1

Result: DEADLOCK</code></pre>
</div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-deadlock-prevention">
<h2>11. Deadlock Prevention, Avoidance, and Recovery</h2>
<h3>Deadlock Prevention</h3>
<p>
        Deadlock prevention ensures that at least
        one necessary deadlock condition cannot occur.
    </p>
<h3>Deadlock Avoidance</h3>
<p>
        Deadlock avoidance evaluates resource requests
        to ensure the system remains in a safe state.
    </p>
<h3>Banker's Algorithm</h3>
<p>
        The Banker's Algorithm checks whether resource
        allocation can allow all processes to complete
        in some safe execution sequence.
    </p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Structure</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Available</td>
<td>Currently available resources</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Max</td>
<td>Maximum process resource demands</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Allocation</td>
<td>Currently allocated resources</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Need</td>
<td>Remaining resources required</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>Need = Max - Allocation</code></pre>
</div>
<h3>Deadlock Recovery</h3>
<p>
        Recovery may involve terminating processes
        or reclaiming resources after detecting
        a deadlock.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-memory-management">
<h2>12. Memory Management: Contiguous Allocation</h2>
<p>
        Memory management controls the allocation,
        translation, and protection of memory
        used by executing processes.
    </p>
<h3>Logical and Physical Addresses</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Logical Address:</strong> Address generated by the CPU.</li>
<li><strong>Physical Address:</strong> Actual location in RAM.</li>
</ul>
<p>
        The Memory Management Unit (MMU) translates
        logical addresses into physical addresses.
    </p>
<h3>Memory Fragmentation</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Internal Fragmentation</td>
<td>Unused space inside allocated blocks.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>External Fragmentation</td>
<td>Free memory scattered into separate holes.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-paging">
<h2>13. Non-Contiguous Memory: Paging Systems</h2>
<p>
        Paging divides logical memory into
        fixed-size pages and physical memory
        into equally sized frames.
    </p>
<p>
        A page table maps logical page numbers
        to physical frame numbers.
    </p>
<h3>Address Translation</h3>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>Logical Address
+-------------+----------+
| Page Number | Offset   |
+-------------+----------+
       |
       v
    Page Table
       |
       v
Physical Address
+-------------+----------+
| Frame Number| Offset   |
+-------------+----------+</code></pre>
</div>
<h3>Translation Lookaside Buffer</h3>
<p>
        The TLB is a small hardware cache
        containing recently used page-to-frame
        translations.
    </p>
<p>
        It reduces the number of memory accesses
        required for address translation.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-advanced-paging">
<h2>14. Advanced Paging Architectures</h2>
<h3>Multi-Level Paging</h3>
<p>
        Multi-level paging divides page tables
        into smaller hierarchical structures
        to reduce memory overhead.
    </p>
<h3>Hashed Page Tables</h3>
<p>
        Hashed page tables use hash functions
        to locate page mappings efficiently
        in large address spaces.
    </p>
<h3>Inverted Page Tables</h3>
<p>
        An inverted page table stores an entry
        for each physical memory frame rather
        than a separate entry for every
        virtual page.
    </p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Method</th>
<th>Key Idea</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Multi-Level</td>
<td>Hierarchical page tables</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Hashed</td>
<td>Hash-based address lookup</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Inverted</td>
<td>One entry per physical frame</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-segmentation">
<h2>15. Memory Segmentation</h2>
<p>
        Segmentation divides a program's logical
        memory into variable-length segments.
    </p>
<h3>Examples of Segments</h3>
<ul>
<li>Main program</li>
<li>Subroutines</li>
<li>Stack</li>
<li>Global data</li>
<li>Symbol table</li>
</ul>
<h3>Segment Address Translation</h3>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>Logical Address:
(Segment Number, Offset)

Segment Table:
Segment Number --&gt; Base + Limit

Physical Address:
Base + Offset</code></pre>
</div>
<p>
        The MMU checks whether an offset is within
        the permitted segment boundary before
        performing memory access.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-virtual-memory">
<h2>16. Virtual Memory Foundations</h2>
<p>
        Virtual memory allows processes to use
        logical address spaces larger than
        available physical RAM.
    </p>
<h3>Demand Paging</h3>
<p>
        Demand paging loads a memory page into RAM
        only when that page is needed.
    </p>
<h3>Page Fault Handling</h3>
<ol>
<li>The CPU references a virtual address.</li>
<li>The MMU detects that the required page is absent.</li>
<li>A page fault trap transfers control to the kernel.</li>
<li>The OS locates the missing page in secondary storage.</li>
<li>The page is loaded into a physical frame.</li>
<li>The page table is updated and execution resumes.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Thrashing</h3>
<p>
        Thrashing occurs when excessive paging activity
        consumes system resources and significantly
        reduces useful processing time.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-page-replacement">
<h2>17. Page Replacement Algorithms</h2>
<p>
        When no free physical memory frame is
        available, the operating system selects
        a page to replace.
    </p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Algorithm</th>
<th>Replacement Rule</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>FIFO</td>
<td>Replace the oldest loaded page.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Optimal</td>
<td>Replace the page used farthest in the future.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>LRU</td>
<td>Replace the least recently used page.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Clock</td>
<td>Use reference bits to grant second chances.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Belady's Anomaly</h3>
<p>
        Under FIFO replacement, increasing the number
        of allocated memory frames can sometimes
        increase the number of page faults.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-file-system">
<h2>18. Storage and File System Abstraction</h2>
<p>
        A file system organizes persistent data
        into files and directories.
    </p>
<h3>Common File Attributes</h3>
<ul>
<li>File name and type</li>
<li>File size</li>
<li>Storage location</li>
<li>Access permissions</li>
<li>Creation and modification timestamps</li>
</ul>
<h3>Directory Structures</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Single-Level:</strong> All files share one directory.</li>
<li><strong>Two-Level:</strong> Each user has a separate directory.</li>
<li><strong>Tree-Structured:</strong> Hierarchical folders.</li>
<li><strong>Acyclic Graph:</strong> Supports shared files or directories.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-file-allocation">
<h2>19. File Allocation Methods</h2>
<p>
        File allocation determines how file
        data blocks are stored on a disk.
    </p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Method</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Contiguous</td>
<td>Stores file blocks consecutively.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Linked</td>
<td>Connects file blocks through pointers.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Indexed</td>
<td>Uses an index containing block addresses.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Indexed Allocation</h3>
<p>
        UNIX-like file systems commonly use
        inode-based structures to manage
        file metadata and block references.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-free-space">
<h2>20. Free Space Management</h2>
<p>
        A file system tracks available storage
        blocks so new files can be allocated
        efficiently.
    </p>
<h3>Free Space Tracking Methods</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bit Vector:</strong> One bit represents each disk block.</li>
<li><strong>Linked List:</strong> Free blocks are connected through pointers.</li>
<li><strong>Grouping:</strong> Free block addresses are stored in groups.</li>
<li><strong>Counting:</strong> Tracks runs of consecutive free blocks.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-disk-scheduling">
<h2>21. Disk Infrastructure and Scheduling</h2>
<h3>Hard Disk Geometry</h3>
<p>
        Traditional hard disks contain rotating
        platters divided into tracks and sectors.
    </p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Seek Time:</strong> Time to move the head to a track.</li>
<li><strong>Rotational Latency:</strong> Time waiting for the target sector.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disk Scheduling Algorithms</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Algorithm</th>
<th>Operation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>FCFS</td>
<td>Serves requests in arrival order.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SSTF</td>
<td>Chooses the closest pending request.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SCAN</td>
<td>Moves back and forth across tracks.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>C-SCAN</td>
<td>Services requests in one sweep direction.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-io-subsystem">
<h2>22. Input/Output Subsystem Architecture</h2>
<p>
        The I/O subsystem manages communication
        between the processor, memory,
        and peripheral devices.
    </p>
<h3>Polling</h3>
<p>
        The CPU repeatedly checks device status
        until an operation completes.
    </p>
<h3>Interrupt-Driven I/O</h3>
<p>
        A device sends an interrupt request
        when it needs processor attention.
    </p>
<h3>Memory-Mapped I/O</h3>
<p>
        Device registers are mapped into the
        processor's address space and accessed
        using memory instructions.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-buffering">
<h2>23. I/O Buffering, Caching, and Spooling</h2>
<h3>Buffering</h3>
<p>
        Buffering temporarily stores data
        during transfers between devices
        operating at different speeds.
    </p>
<h3>Caching</h3>
<p>
        Caching keeps recently accessed data
        in faster memory to improve access speed.
    </p>
<h3>Spooling</h3>
<p>
        Spooling queues operations for devices
        that cannot process multiple requests
        simultaneously.
    </p>
<h3>Printer Spooling Example</h3>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>User A Print Request ---+
                        |
User B Print Request ---+--&gt; Print Queue
                        |        |
User C Print Request ---+        v
                             Printer</code></pre>
</div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-access-control">
<h2>24. Protection and Access Control</h2>
<p>
        Operating systems enforce access restrictions
        to protect files, devices, memory,
        and other resources.
    </p>
<h3>Access Matrix</h3>
<p>
        An access matrix describes which users
        or processes can perform operations
        on particular objects.
    </p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>User</th>
<th>File A</th>
<th>File B</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>User 1</td>
<td>Read / Write</td>
<td>Read</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>User 2</td>
<td>Read</td>
<td>No Access</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Access Control Lists</h3>
<p>
        ACLs associate permission entries
        with individual protected objects.
    </p>
<h3>Capability Lists</h3>
<p>
        Capability lists associate access
        rights with a user or process domain.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-security">
<h2>25. System Security and Threat Mitigation</h2>
<p>
        Operating system security protects
        system resources from unauthorized
        access, malicious software,
        and other security threats.
    </p>
<h3>Authentication</h3>
<p>
        Authentication verifies the identity
        of a user or system entity.
    </p>
<h3>Authorization</h3>
<p>
        Authorization determines what an
        authenticated entity is permitted to do.
    </p>
<h3>Principle of Least Privilege</h3>
<p>
        Users and processes should receive
        only the permissions necessary
        to perform their assigned tasks.
    </p>
<h3>Access Control Models</h3>
<ul>
<li>Discretionary Access Control (DAC)</li>
<li>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-virtualization">
<h2>26. Virtualization and Hypervisors</h2>
<p>
        Virtualization allows multiple
        isolated virtual machines to
        share the hardware of one computer.
    </p>
<h3>Hypervisors</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Type 1</td>
<td>Runs directly on physical hardware.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Type 2</td>
<td>Runs as software on a host operating system.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Examples</h3>
<ul>
<li>VMware ESXi</li>
<li>Xen</li>
<li>VirtualBox</li>
<li>VMware Workstation</li>
</ul>
<h3>Containers</h3>
<p>
        Containers isolate application environments
        while sharing the host operating system kernel.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-multiprocessing">
<h2>27. Distributed and Multiprocessor Operating Systems</h2>
<h3>Symmetric Multiprocessing (SMP)</h3>
<p>
        SMP allows multiple processors or
        CPU cores to share memory and
        operate under one operating system.
    </p>
<h3>Non-Uniform Memory Access (NUMA)</h3>
<p>
        NUMA architectures have memory
        access times that depend on
        the location of memory relative
        to the processor.
    </p>
<h3>Distributed File Systems</h3>
<p>
        Distributed file systems allow
        data to be accessed across
        multiple network-connected computers.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-real-time">
<h2>28. Real-Time Operating Systems</h2>
<p>
        A real-time operating system is
        designed to execute important
        tasks within predictable timing
        constraints.
    </p>
<h3>Hard Real-Time Systems</h3>
<p>
        Missing a critical deadline is
        considered a system failure.
    </p>
<h3>Soft Real-Time Systems</h3>
<p>
        Missing a deadline reduces
        service quality but does not
        necessarily cause complete failure.
    </p>
<h3>Real-Time Scheduling</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rate-Monotonic:</strong> Fixed priorities based on task periods.</li>
<li><strong>Earliest Deadline First:</strong> Prioritizes the nearest deadline.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="os-modern-frontiers">
<h2>29. Modern Operating System Frontiers and Mobile Kernels</h2>
<p>
        Modern operating systems support
        mobile devices, cloud environments,
        hardware virtualization, and
        advanced security mechanisms.
    </p>
<h3>Android Binder</h3>
<p>
        Binder is an interprocess
        communication mechanism used
        in Android systems.
    </p>
<h3>Apple Darwin</h3>
<p>
        Darwin provides the core operating
        system foundation used by Apple's
        operating systems.
    </p>
<h3>Extended Berkeley Packet Filter (eBPF)</h3>
<p>
        eBPF enables verified programs to
        execute in the Linux kernel for
        networking, monitoring, and
        other supported tasks.
    </p>
<h3>Conclusion</h3>
<p>
        Operating systems coordinate
        processing, memory, storage,
        input/output, and security
        to provide stable environments
        for application software.
    </p>
</section>
